#!/usr/bin/env python3
from pathlib import Path
import re, sys, difflib

path=Path("compression-without-verification.tex")
if not path.is_file(): sys.exit(f"Missing {path}; run in ~/laboratory/working")
original=path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
s=original

SECTION = "\\section{Verification Across the Lifecycle}\n\\label{sec:lifecycle}\n\nThe earlier section on deferred verification located correctness at the\nlevel of aggregate evaluation: individual records are admitted unverified,\nand the resulting policy is later judged on independently tested problems.\nThat arrangement is only as strong as the evaluator and as the path by\nwhich a bad update can be corrected. Four papers from October 2026 make\nboth of these measurable and, taken together, refine the thesis from a\nrequirement to verify before compressing into a requirement that spans the\nwhole life of a consequential change.\n\n\\subsection{Verifier strength and verifier pressure}\n\nWang and Qi define verifier strength as the agreement between an\navailable admission verifier, often a language-model judge, and a trusted\nreference verifier, and propose a gate with three outcomes: admit, reject,\nand refuse when strength is unmeasured or exact rollback is unavailable\n\\cite{wang2026recoverability}. Their central empirical case is a silent\ncollapse. A deployed judge intended to score both dataset breadth and\nimbalance returned exactly 0.250 across all 40 runs in its deployed\nconfiguration, ignoring imbalance without emitting any error, and complete\ncollapse occurred in 9 of 37 configurations, while larger judges\nresponded to the same signal. Two inexpensive intervention tests, removing\nthe purported signal and checking that request-level settings took\neffect, detected the problem. The lesson for the present argument is that\naggregate consequential verification is not self-certifying: a verifier\nthat returns a constant certifies nothing, and a pipeline can run to\ncompletion on it.\n\nLee, Kim, and Park add a companion quantity, verifier pressure, the ratio\nof the verifier's contribution to a search objective to the variation in\nthe primary performance objective \\cite{lee2026ownverifier}. When this\nratio is far below one, a verifier that appears to survive optimization\nhas shown little, because it may simply have been too weak to steer the\nsearch. In their self-audit the original configuration had pressure of\nroughly 0.007 to 0.089, partly because an accuracy gate silently reduced\nthe verifier's nominal weight, so the earlier negative result was a\ndiscovery failure and not evidence of robustness. The strongest failure\noccurred when the agent could query the verifier before committing: it\nretained claims that passed the in-loop check while held-out faithfulness\ndeclined, which is selection against the measurement boundary and not\nimproved correspondence.\n\nBoth findings bear on the evaluation described earlier in this essay. The\nSSD paper selects some reported results as the best across checkpoints\nagainst a small primary benchmark, and checkpoint selection of that kind is\na mild form of pre-commit access to the evaluator. This does not impugn\nthe qualitative finding, which is large and consistent, but it means that\nthe benchmark's role as the third-level judgment is weaker for any single\nreported configuration than for the method as a whole. The control the\ntwo papers recommend is cheap to state: before interpreting a result as\nrobustness under optimization, demonstrate that the evaluator exerted\nenough causal pressure for gaming it to have been detectable, and report\nselection-flip rates alongside any aggregate.\n\n\\subsection{State recoverability and trajectory recoverability}\n\nSSD is a weight update, which is the category Wang and Qi keep behind human\napproval because a learned verifier can fail silently there. Their\ndistinction between two kinds of recoverability applies directly.\n\\emph{State recoverability} is the ability to reinstate the former model,\nprompt, tool, or skill. \\emph{Trajectory recoverability} is the ability to\nundo the consequences produced while the new version was active. In their\nexperiments restoring a saved version recovered the old behavior, whereas\nasking the system to regenerate an undo did not, and a change that was\nlater reverted still altered outcomes on 17\\% of tasks beyond ordinary\nrun-to-run variation while it was deployed \\cite{wang2026recoverability}.\nThe evidence is preliminary: one platform, a 50-task airline-domain\nexperiment, and small numbers of communication-sensitive cases, with no\nstudy of repository-scale coding, accumulated chains of individually\nreversible edits, or weight-update recovery under changed data and\noptimizer state.\n\nFor compression, state recoverability is the easy half. Retaining the base\ncheckpoint restores the previous distribution exactly. What retention\ncannot restore is the record of programs, decisions, and downstream\ntraining data produced by the compressed model before the defect was\nnoticed. A reversible representation change and a causally reversible\ndeployment are therefore different properties, and the verification\nboundary should be drawn after both: verified admission does not complete a\ncompression claim unless exposure before correction is also bounded.\n\n\\subsection{Attribution and the positive boundary}\n\nTwo of the four papers mark the edges of the thesis from opposite sides.\nTerminal-RSI reports stagewise gains across five training stages of one\n27B model, but stage-specific rewards, learning rates, initialization,\nrewritten data, and critic inheritance all change together, and the authors\nacknowledge missing controls including independent seeds, a compute-matched\nbaseline, and a clean inherited-versus-reset-critic comparison\n\\cite{yang2026terminalrsi}. It is best described as benchmark improvement\nunder evolving supervision. It does not identify recursive rewriting or\ncritic inheritance as the cause, and later success does not show that\nearlier competence was preserved or responsible, which is the attribution\ngap this essay has argued for throughout.\n\nPAR is the one result that qualifies the thesis and does not merely\nstrengthen it. By training a looped model so that recurrent iteration $k$\nrepresents the state after operation $k$, and by using prefix\nself-distillation to supply intermediate targets, it reaches 95.5\\%\nfull-state accuracy at 512 operations after training on at most 32\n\\cite{wang2026par}. That is genuine extrapolative transfer, and it is\nobtained by self-distillation. But it occurs where compression is densely\nverified: the task has a stable compositional transition, progress aligns\nexactly with computation depth, and correctness can be checked at every\nintermediate state. Its scope is also narrow, since the headline result\nholds for one small model, larger Qwen2.5 models show standard deviations\nof 33 to 43 points at the longest horizon, and the tasks are synthetic\nfinite-state transformations. PAR is therefore the boundary case and not a\ncounterexample. Where intermediate semantics are specified and exhaustively\ncheckable, much stronger transfer claims are justified, and the compressed\nstate is continually constrained by a known correspondence. SSD on\ncompetitive programming has no such correspondence, which is why its\nresults support the narrower reading of reorganized access.\n\nThe refinement these papers support can be stated as four conditions that\nmust hold together for a consequential transformation to count as\nverified: the verifier's causal strength has been measured and shown to\nexceed the pressure the optimization applied to it, an exact prior state\nhas been preserved, exposure before correction is bounded, and the claimed\ncorrespondence between the compressed object and the task is\nre-established after the transformation. No paper this week demonstrates\nrepository-scale coding capability faithfully compressed into a smaller\nindependent model, and the essay's claims remain about reorganization of\nreachable behavior, not about transfer of verified competence."
ABSTRACT = 'The essay further argues, drawing on four October 2026 papers, that aggregate evaluation is itself a verifier whose strength and causal pressure must be measured, that restoring a prior model state does not undo consequences produced while the new one was deployed, and that dense intermediate verification marks the setting in which self-distillation transfers most strongly.'
LINK = 'Section~\\ref{sec:lifecycle} examines what this deferred judgment requires of the evaluator and of the path for correcting a bad update.'
CONCLUSION = "Verified admission does not complete the compression claim. One must also measure the verifier's causal strength, preserve an exact prior state, bound exposure before correction, and re-establish the claimed correspondence after every consequential transformation."
BIB = "\\bibitem{wang2026recoverability}\nWang and Qi.\n\\newblock Recoverability and Verifier Strength: Two Measured Preconditions\nfor Unattended Agent Self-Improvement.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2610.27465}, 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{lee2026ownverifier}\nLee, Kim, and Park.\n\\newblock When Can You Trust an Agent's Own Verifier?\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2610.22779}, 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{yang2026terminalrsi}\nYang et al.\n\\newblock Terminal-RSI: Recursive Self-Improving Terminal Agents.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2610.22192}, 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{wang2026par}\nWang et al.\n\\newblock PAR: Progress-Aligned Recurrence for Length Generalization in\nState Tracking.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2610.27628}, 2026."

def one(pattern, replacement, label, flags=0):
    global s
    hits=list(re.finditer(pattern,s,flags))
    if len(hits)!=1: sys.exit(f"STOP: {label}: expected 1 match, got {len(hits)}. No changes made.")
    m=hits[0]
    s=s[:m.start()]+replacement+s[m.end():]

if "\\section{Verification Across the Lifecycle}" in s:
    sys.exit("STOP: lifecycle section already present")

one(r"(?=\\section\{Limitations and Proposed Experiments\})", SECTION+"\n\n", "new section")

# Insert at end of abstract, preserving original text.
one(r"(?=\\end\{abstract\})", "\n"+ABSTRACT+"\n", "abstract")

# Place cross-reference immediately before next section.
one(r"(?=\\section\{What Kind of Claim Is This\?\})", LINK+"\n\n", "deferred-verification cross-reference")

# Place concluding sentence before bibliography, or document end if no bibliography.
m=re.search(r"\\section\{Conclusion\}(.*?)(?=\\begin\{thebibliography\}|\\bibliography\{|\\end\{document\})",s,re.S)
if not m: sys.exit("STOP: cannot locate conclusion boundary; no changes made")
pos=m.end()
s=s[:pos].rstrip()+"\n\n"+CONCLUSION+"\n\n"+s[pos:]

if "\\begin{thebibliography}" in s:
    one(r"(?=\\end\{thebibliography\})", BIB+"\n\n", "bibliography")
elif re.search(r"\\bibliography\{",s):
    sys.exit("STOP: BibTeX bibliography detected; add entries to .bib instead. No changes made")
else:
    one(r"(?=\\end\{document\})", "\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n"+BIB+"\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n", "new bibliography")

backup=path.with_suffix('.tex.bak')
if backup.exists(): sys.exit(f"STOP: backup exists: {backup}. No changes made")
backup.write_text(original,encoding='utf-8')
path.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
print('Updated',path,'; backup:',backup)
print(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(original.splitlines(True),s.splitlines(True),fromfile=str(backup),tofile=str(path))))

